joining me now to talk about a campaign  for the mayor of henderson dan stewart  thank you for joining us dan well thank  you john for having me it's a pleasure  to be here currently of course on the  henderson city council uh  first question why do you want to be  mayor well i think  probably most important two things even  most importantly is i think we've we've  reached a critical point in the growth  and evolution of our city  that i think we really need some  sophisticated  thought and planning  to uh to take us into that next step but  most importantly  i've got to tell you i want to uh  lead the charge in taking back control  of our schools for the city of anderson  and we'll we'll touch on that in a  moment i know it's it's an issue not  only yourself but your your opponent  in the race is mentioned as well but you  mentioned you're at sort of a critical  point for the city of henderson  what  is the  what kind of a path would you like to  see charted for the city when it comes  to the development when it comes to  making sure that  the city continues on the track that  it's on now or do you see a different  track well that's where i think is  important that we make sure we are  totally and completely transparent and  we get the input of the  residents now because  they have every right to be involved and  have a big say in the way we go in in  the path we take but  for me it is to keep the hometown feel  that we have right now that we've built  over the last 30 or 40 years we really  it's a fabulous community the leaders  before us  the folks officials have really  really made a great community but we're  at that kind of critical point to where  do we want to continue that way do we  need to grow how how is that growth  going to take place do you feel like  with the growth of  west henderson  obviously we talk about green valley and  all the residential developments there  on the south end of the beltway  is henderson losing that neighborhood  feel that you're referring to i don't  think so i i think that we can maintain  in fact i know we can maintain that  that neighborhood that community feel  uh and still yet grow and become that  more sophisticated community that we  need to be so how do you do that in  terms of what projects come in what  businesses you bring in what is what  does that look like well  as i've said  we're a great community lots of good  things have been done so we have the  foundation to continue that growth  but it needs to be done in a very  strategic  thoughtful way so that we can be very  selective actually in the businesses  that we try and attract and folks that  we try and  come and be part of our community  that can share our vision sure  you mentioned the school district you  started a pac i believe  yeah there's  you are uh  determined to separate henderson school  district for henderson from ccsd  uh  why  well  the easy answer  which is the truth is ccsd is just too  big it's just become  unyielding it just  it's we can't get anything done it's too  big  the  it's just a morass of  red tape and we need to have local  control  parents need to be able to be more  involved with their own with their own  children and their in the children's  education and the only way to do that is  to make school districts smaller  um  you're not the only person who's brought  this up this idea before there has been  an effort by for years to separate ccsd  and obviously uh council member romero  has brought this idea up as well  why is there such a push in henderson to  be on your own from ccsd well i think uh  i think we've reached that again i hate  to keep using that word that critical  point i think pandemic has kind of been  the straw that broke the camel's  camel's back i think there's so much  frustration amongst parents that people  are just in the outcry that we've got to  have more control yes you're right john  this has been something since  my kids were going to school i'm a ccsd  product my kids are a ccsd product my  grandkids are a ccsd product so i  understand this but we've been talking  about it and talking about it and  talking about it's time to do something  about it how do you do something about  it that's it's not like you can just  like flip this  it's going to be hard and arduous but  i'm up for the task we've we've started  the pack  community schools initiative  and uh anybody that  wants to help us we need all the help we  can get can go to community schools  nevada envy excuse me community schools  nv is our website but it's going to be  long it's going to be hard but it's  worth the effort and  it'll either be it will be done through  initiative the ballot or through the  legislature  you've discussed uh the issues of  transparency when it comes to growth you  discuss the effort to separate ccsd  from ccsd  i spoke with councilmember romero you  know of course and she brought up  many of the same things  so if i'm a voter why would i vote for  you instead of her because i'm actually  putting together this pact i'm actually  raising the money to  make this happen to go out and get this  necessary signatures to get this  initiative qualified it's just not an  easy process  like you said  you know snapping your fingers it's a  long process but i've started that  process with this  with this pack with this committee  and we are moving forward we're going to  get it done and it's the only way i know  of doing it is taking it to the people  getting the uh getting the initiative  qualified and then getting the  necessary signatures  just to elaborate a little more if  possible on  how  you  as the campaigns go on how do you plan  to differentiate yourself from your  opponent considering if you look just  sort of you know one against one a lot  of your positions are pretty similar in  terms of your vision for the future the  economic development aspect the school  district aspect public safety there's  not a whole lot of difference is there  so how there's huge difference so tell  me about what they are the perspective i  have been 40 45 years in this valley and  i'm a businessman i come from a total  businessman's point of view business  person's point of view excuse me and we  you know that perspective is necessary  to get these hard things done  and  perspective is everything i come from  the taxpayer i come from a customer of  government i've worked through  government and the red tape my entire  life so there is a huge difference  between a public sector  point of view and a private sector point  of view which my point of view is that's  where i come from why do you believe for  the overall city of henderson a private  sector viewpoint is best  i think because it's the way to solve  problems i think that the private sector  in the past has always been able to step  up  and able to  well let me let me let me put it in a  little different way my my strength my  strength i believe is being able to  bring people together  get a consensus solve the problems move  forward and i think the private sector  does that a lot better  is there a place for government  absolutely i'm part of that that  solution as well but at the same time  you need that perspective the business  person's perspective  on how to get things done  how much of mayor march's  agenda and the current city council sort  of uh  way of maybe thinking or the way the  votes have been going etc  how much of a change from the current  course of action do you anticipate if  you become mayor  is it status quo or is it okay i'm mayor  stewart  we're going to chart a little bit of a  different course here um that's that's a  very good question i'm not one to come  in and  wave my arms i'm saying we need to  change we need to change i think we need  to course correct a little bit how so  just elaborate that a little bit more if  you can again back to that point of  becoming a little more sophisticated  what does that mean sophisticated i know  what it means but like in terms of city  development  uh  sustainable growth not just sustainable  growth but and development but it's a  sustainable community how do we  continue to be the henderson that we are  and that we love and we love being part  of  but how do we anticipate and plan for  the future growth growth is coming we  just need to make sure we do it in the  right proper way  so you ask the question how do we become  sustainable in terms of growth what's  the answer  smart intelligent  thinking and planning  and  collaboration with the right kind of  people and attracting the right kind of  businesses  uh final question just sort of the  number one priority in your view for  henderson moving forward  take control of the schools  our own schools that's that's my mission  that's what gets me up in the morning  i'm passionate about it uh we're going  to make this happen and that's just not  for henderson this is for the state of  nevada i think for the especially for  this  southern nevada  we it's not just for the kids in  henderson it's for the kids and the  students throughout southern nevada it's  just got to be done ccsd is too big  we need smaller school districts just to  quickly follow up on that again we've  seen recently the issue at lamping  elementary in henderson  what is the correlation in your view  between what's happened there in the  last since school started essentially  and what you want to try and do can i be  personal can you use a personal example  my daughter lives in omaha there are  smaller school districts back there  they in one week because their school  district's smaller and they have better  control and one week they decided are we  going to wear masks or we're not going  to wear masks all right right now you  couldn't get that done in  you know with a buy-in from everybody  so i think with  the smaller school district  better control  parents more involved parents are the  key to a child's education and so  smaller school district  affords and allows people parents  families to become more involved and  therefore get the solutions quicker and  better solutions all right well good  luck on your campaign sir we look  forward to talking with you a little bit  later on appreciate it well john thanks  for having me it's been a pleasure

